WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:07.173
The amount of smoke coming
out of it, like a barbecue,

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:09.259
that is not something that you would see.

00:00:09.259 --> 00:00:10.760
When it hits the ground,

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:12.095
it's actually really cold.

00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:14.347
I am professor Gretchen Benedix.

00:00:14.347 --> 00:00:16.641
I have been studying meteorites

00:00:16.641 --> 00:00:20.020
and asteroids for about 20 years.

00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:21.229
Today, we're going to be looking

00:00:21.229 --> 00:00:24.107
at asteroid disasters in movies and on TV

00:00:24.107 --> 00:00:25.650
and judge how real they are.

00:00:30.238 --> 00:00:33.033
Now, we can get asteroids
that are fully metallic,

00:00:33.033 --> 00:00:33.867
but they wouldn't be spiky
the way this thing looks,

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:34.743
with all the canyons and things.

00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:36.411
The asteroids that we do see

00:00:36.411 --> 00:00:39.205
do not have this kind of
debris cloud around them.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:42.500
An asteroid will generally not have

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.544
too much of this gas coming off of it.

00:00:44.544 --> 00:00:46.046
It may have some ice in it;

00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:47.630
that's not going to be the majority of it.

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:52.761
I'm pretty sure that we would see

00:00:52.761 --> 00:00:54.804
something that size headed for us

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:56.514
well before it got to that point.

00:00:56.514 --> 00:00:57.891
Very unrealistic.

00:01:03.605 --> 00:01:04.814
The biggest myth is 

00:01:04.814 --> 00:01:08.026
that you actually have
to steer your spaceship

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:09.527
to avoid asteroids.

00:01:09.527 --> 00:01:11.071
There's a ton of bodies out there.

00:01:11.071 --> 00:01:12.739
It's a huge amount of space.

00:01:12.739 --> 00:01:16.409
It's really hard to get into a situation

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:18.536
where you might hit some accidentally.

00:01:23.875 --> 00:01:25.919
You could potentially land on it.

00:01:25.919 --> 00:01:27.504
It's not like landing on Earth.

00:01:27.504 --> 00:01:30.924
You're dealing with a totally
different gravity regime.

00:01:30.924 --> 00:01:33.510
You know, we've had a couple
of really tiny asteroids

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.679
that we've got missions
from Japan and the US,

00:01:36.679 --> 00:01:39.390
and they have these
little touchdown things,

00:01:39.390 --> 00:01:41.184
and it's really, really, really hard

00:01:41.184 --> 00:01:44.479
just to do this tiny
little drone-size thing

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:46.731
and land it down on the
surface of the asteroid

00:01:46.731 --> 00:01:47.899
and then get it back off.

00:01:51.903 --> 00:01:55.490
The amount of power you'd
need to actually explode,

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:56.491
it is impossible.

00:01:56.491 --> 00:01:59.828
We don't have enough explosive
material on the Earth.

00:01:59.828 --> 00:02:03.414
So, there have been some studies
done that have shown that

00:02:03.414 --> 00:02:07.377
if you take an asteroid
20 kilometers across,

00:02:07.377 --> 00:02:11.297
you need something like
200 gigatons of explosion

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:13.800
to break that apart completely.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.512
Now, the biggest thing that
we've ever exploded on Earth

00:02:17.512 --> 00:02:19.305
was 50 megatons.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:22.308
So you'd need 4,000 of those

00:02:22.308 --> 00:02:24.644
to blow up something 20 kilometers across.

00:02:24.644 --> 00:02:27.564
This thing in this movie
is 1,000 kilometers across.

00:02:27.564 --> 00:02:32.569
You'd need 130,000 15-megaton bombs

00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:35.530
to try and blow that
particular asteroid up.

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:40.827
A shock wave, it's a pressure sound wave.

00:02:40.827 --> 00:02:43.163
You need atmosphere to have sound.

00:02:43.163 --> 00:02:45.999
So when the sound is playing in the movie

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:47.625
about this explosion happening,

00:02:47.625 --> 00:02:49.460
that's possible because there is a lot

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:51.713
of what we can see is atmosphere there.

00:02:51.713 --> 00:02:54.174
So there's some gas molecules,

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:56.968
and that's going to cause
that sound wave to travel.

00:02:56.968 --> 00:02:58.386
But it's space,

00:02:58.386 --> 00:03:00.555
so usually there's a whole lot of space

00:03:00.555 --> 00:03:02.932
in between all of these molecules,

00:03:02.932 --> 00:03:06.728
so the sound is not going to
be what we hear here on Earth.

00:03:06.728 --> 00:03:09.981
You will have a shock wave,
but it won't travel out

00:03:09.981 --> 00:03:11.441
both sides of a thing.

00:03:11.441 --> 00:03:13.234
It'll only travel out one direction.

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:15.778
That's not how you would actually try to

00:03:15.778 --> 00:03:17.947
destroy an asteroid that's coming for you.

00:03:17.947 --> 00:03:20.700
So I would give it a 1 out of 10.

00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:27.165
65 million years ago

00:03:27.165 --> 00:03:29.292
is not when the asteroid got knocked out

00:03:29.292 --> 00:03:30.752
of the asteroid belt.

00:03:30.752 --> 00:03:33.296
That is when the asteroid
actually hit the Earth.

00:03:33.296 --> 00:03:36.633
No. 2 wrong, the asteroid
belt does not look like that.

00:03:36.633 --> 00:03:40.511
It's not a doughnut of rocks
that are that close together.

00:03:40.511 --> 00:03:42.680
Sometimes you can get an asteroid family.

00:03:42.680 --> 00:03:44.599
So you can get one asteroid that has

00:03:44.599 --> 00:03:47.268
maybe three or four other
asteroids kind of close to it.

00:03:47.268 --> 00:03:48.811
So, this asteroid gets knocked out.

00:03:48.811 --> 00:03:51.189
You can have collisions
every once in a while.

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:53.650
They do get the size of the rock correct.

00:03:53.650 --> 00:03:57.320
Over time, the sizes of the
rocks that are out in space

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:58.279
have actually gotten smaller,

00:03:58.279 --> 00:03:59.822
because they do run into each other,

00:03:59.822 --> 00:04:01.950
but they're really far away from Earth.

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:03.826
So not all of them are going to get

00:04:03.826 --> 00:04:06.371
onto a collision course with Earth,

00:04:06.371 --> 00:04:09.624
and they don't necessarily
impart enough energy

00:04:09.624 --> 00:04:12.585
to stick it on a direct collision course

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:14.212
to the inner solar system.

00:04:14.212 --> 00:04:16.381
The collisions that occur out there

00:04:16.381 --> 00:04:19.133
over time have gotten fewer and fewer,

00:04:19.133 --> 00:04:23.972
because we've kind of gone
from a really chaotic system

00:04:23.972 --> 00:04:27.308
4 1/2 billion years ago to
now it's pretty quiescent.

00:04:27.308 --> 00:04:29.519
There's a mission called DART.

00:04:29.519 --> 00:04:31.646
They're trying to take a small asteroid.

00:04:31.646 --> 00:04:34.482
They're going to push it
with their little spacecraft

00:04:34.482 --> 00:04:37.193
and see if it will change
its orbit in any way.

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.534
Then the movie takes a
different tact and says,

00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:45.660
well, it missed.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.745
The orbit was such that
it kind of got close,

00:04:47.745 --> 00:04:49.497
but it flew by.

00:04:49.497 --> 00:04:51.624
Now, we do have objects that fly by.

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:52.750
That's kind of cool.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:55.920
And that, I don't think a 6-mile asteroid

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:58.047
would actually skim the atmosphere

00:04:58.047 --> 00:04:59.632
and then bounce out again.

00:04:59.632 --> 00:05:01.884
But you can do that with
smaller-sized objects.

00:05:01.884 --> 00:05:04.887
Again, 1 out of 10 for realism.

00:05:08.599 --> 00:05:09.475
I love this show.

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:12.020
And in terms of the realism
about everything else,

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.273
they actually use real
asteroid shape files.

00:05:15.273 --> 00:05:18.026
They have coated it with material

00:05:18.026 --> 00:05:19.527
that would make it hard to see.

00:05:19.527 --> 00:05:22.530
The amount of light that's
reflecting off the surface

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:24.907
is the only way we can tell
that that thing is there.

00:05:24.907 --> 00:05:26.075
So we can see it moving,

00:05:26.075 --> 00:05:28.703
and if it's moving fast
and it's bright enough,

00:05:28.703 --> 00:05:30.997
the amount of light it
reflects from the sun

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:32.540
is how we tell it's there.

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:36.419
So if they've coated it with
this coating that's stealthy,

00:05:36.419 --> 00:05:38.379
then system that they have in place

00:05:38.379 --> 00:05:42.175
to detect incoming
asteroids, that would fail.

00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:43.760
So that actually makes sense.

00:05:49.474 --> 00:05:52.810
I would be surprised if he
didn't hear it, to be honest,

00:05:52.810 --> 00:05:56.272
because that would be a
very loud rumbly noise

00:05:56.272 --> 00:05:59.359
that you would kind of notice
before you saw the light.

00:05:59.359 --> 00:06:02.487
There have been fireballs
that have come across.

00:06:02.487 --> 00:06:03.571
We get images of them,

00:06:03.571 --> 00:06:05.990
and people don't hear them or see them.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:07.867
Or sometimes people will hear something

00:06:07.867 --> 00:06:09.911
but never see the fireball itself.

00:06:09.911 --> 00:06:12.330
For this particular scene,
all of that makes sense.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:19.754
You'd feel the thermal radiation

00:06:19.754 --> 00:06:21.881
before you felt the shock wave.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:23.841
That's definitely realistic.

00:06:23.841 --> 00:06:27.762
So even if you're, like,
500 kilometers away,

00:06:27.762 --> 00:06:32.392
you might feel the thermal
radiation in maybe a minute,

00:06:32.392 --> 00:06:34.477
whereas you might not feel the shock wave

00:06:34.477 --> 00:06:37.480
for half an hour or more, an hour.

00:06:37.480 --> 00:06:41.818
And the effects of the thermal
radiation would be burning.

00:06:41.818 --> 00:06:44.987
Your clothing could burn
off. Your skin might not.

00:06:44.987 --> 00:06:47.323
But I imagine that it
would, it would just depend

00:06:47.323 --> 00:06:49.909
on how far away from the impact you are.

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:51.869
So at least an 8 out of 10 for realism.

00:06:55.832 --> 00:06:56.749
Pause on the comet.

00:06:56.749 --> 00:06:58.376
Let's talk about that first.

00:06:58.376 --> 00:07:00.962
There's this whole bunch of smaller bodies

00:07:00.962 --> 00:07:03.965
in between Mars and Jupiter
that we called asteroids,

00:07:03.965 --> 00:07:07.760
and asteroids are really
rocky metallic bodies.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:10.513
Comets come from farther
out in the solar system.

00:07:10.513 --> 00:07:13.766
Comets are composed
primarily of ice and dust.

00:07:13.766 --> 00:07:15.893
The fireball that we're seeing here,

00:07:15.893 --> 00:07:18.896
these bits of material
are fragmenting off,

00:07:18.896 --> 00:07:21.274
and they are still moving fast enough

00:07:21.274 --> 00:07:24.861
so that they can exhibit a glow as well.

00:07:24.861 --> 00:07:27.280
That is a normal thing that you would see,

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.826
just not at this size and distance.

00:07:31.826 --> 00:07:34.203
The size of this rock
is about the same size

00:07:34.203 --> 00:07:37.832
as the rock that caused the
extinction of the dinosaurs.

00:07:37.832 --> 00:07:40.585
So having shelters is a good idea,

00:07:40.585 --> 00:07:42.795
because we know what that asteroid did.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:45.840
We'd want to be underground.

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:50.052
Potentially could devolve into that level,

00:07:50.052 --> 00:07:51.679
of governments making choices

00:07:51.679 --> 00:07:54.056
about randomly picking people to save

00:07:54.056 --> 00:07:56.767
and then finding shelters
for them to go into.

00:08:02.190 --> 00:08:04.108
What we're seeing is that the entire Earth

00:08:04.108 --> 00:08:07.028
has been wrecked and poisoned.

00:08:07.028 --> 00:08:11.532
There would be potentially
a global dust layer

00:08:11.532 --> 00:08:12.617
on top of everything.

00:08:12.617 --> 00:08:15.077
But you would not
necessarily see the level

00:08:15.077 --> 00:08:18.080
of shock damage or structural damage

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:22.001
that is being shown here for
every place on the planet.

00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:24.670
You could potentially
have sunlight be blocked

00:08:24.670 --> 00:08:28.341
for quite a while, and then
that could settle down.

00:08:28.341 --> 00:08:29.884
2 or 3 out of 10.

00:08:29.884 --> 00:08:32.470
We've never witnessed something this size

00:08:32.470 --> 00:08:33.971
hit a terrestrial planet.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:40.561
What we're seeing is the smoke trail.

00:08:40.561 --> 00:08:43.397
We're not actually seeing the fireball.

00:08:43.397 --> 00:08:45.066
And then an explosion.

00:08:45.066 --> 00:08:46.734
That's not how it works.

00:08:46.734 --> 00:08:50.071
It would be something
that is like a fireball

00:08:50.071 --> 00:08:51.864
and then a trail of smoke behind it.

00:08:51.864 --> 00:08:53.032
You know, we have evidence of this.

00:08:53.032 --> 00:08:57.995
In 2015, this
several-tens-of-meters-wide object

00:08:57.995 --> 00:09:00.665
came through the atmosphere in Russia,

00:09:00.665 --> 00:09:02.416
and it fragmented,

00:09:02.416 --> 00:09:05.836
and people caught the
whole thing on dash cams.

00:09:08.923 --> 00:09:12.093
So, the impact itself
would potentially cause

00:09:12.093 --> 00:09:13.636
a lot of material to come out.

00:09:13.636 --> 00:09:15.346
I don't think it would
go straight up like that.

00:09:15.346 --> 00:09:18.349
It would more spill out sideways,

00:09:18.349 --> 00:09:20.643
and then the shock wave would come after.

00:09:20.643 --> 00:09:22.895
Certain impacts can burst your eardrums

00:09:22.895 --> 00:09:24.397
because of the airburst afterwards.

00:09:24.397 --> 00:09:25.648
So if it's big enough, yeah,

00:09:25.648 --> 00:09:28.317
you can get noise levels that are

00:09:28.317 --> 00:09:31.445
well beyond breaking your eardrums.

00:09:31.445 --> 00:09:33.781
And that's the way the cookie crumbles!

00:09:33.781 --> 00:09:35.950
There's a trail,

00:09:35.950 --> 00:09:38.828
a skidded track to the end of the trail.

00:09:38.828 --> 00:09:40.746
No. That doesn't happen with impacts.

00:09:40.746 --> 00:09:42.915
Impacts are an all-or-nothing thing.

00:09:42.915 --> 00:09:46.210
Even if they come in at
a really steep angle,

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:48.713
it will still make a single hole,

00:09:48.713 --> 00:09:52.258
and it won't have a gouge in
the Earth leading up to it.

00:09:52.258 --> 00:09:54.802
That's gonna be a zero on this one.

00:09:54.802 --> 00:09:56.971
Does that look like a problem to you?

00:09:59.515 --> 00:10:00.933
Meteor shower!

00:10:00.933 --> 00:10:02.810
They call this a meteor shower.

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.813
The size of that thing is not a meteor.

00:10:05.813 --> 00:10:06.981
Those are big rocks.

00:10:06.981 --> 00:10:09.191
And we now know meteors are really tiny,

00:10:09.191 --> 00:10:11.527
small specks of dust.

00:10:11.527 --> 00:10:15.573
So meteors are different than asteroids

00:10:15.573 --> 00:10:18.618
and comets and meteorites.

00:10:18.618 --> 00:10:20.995
We're talking about tiny dust particles

00:10:20.995 --> 00:10:23.414
that are between the size of sand grains

00:10:23.414 --> 00:10:25.458
to maybe a small pebble size

00:10:25.458 --> 00:10:27.251
that are burning up in the atmosphere.

00:10:27.251 --> 00:10:28.544
So this is a fireball,

00:10:28.544 --> 00:10:30.963
or we could even call it a bolide,

00:10:30.963 --> 00:10:33.049
if we're feeling super scientific.

00:10:37.928 --> 00:10:41.390
If you see a fireball and it
looks like it's headed for you,

00:10:41.390 --> 00:10:44.810
stand there until the
very last possible second,

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:46.854
because if it's small,

00:10:46.854 --> 00:10:49.106
you might be able to jump out of the way.

00:10:49.106 --> 00:10:50.983
But probably not.

00:10:50.983 --> 00:10:53.402
And if it's big, it doesn't matter.

00:10:53.402 --> 00:10:54.362
I get this a lot.

00:10:54.362 --> 00:10:57.865
"I saw a fireball over there,
so it must have just landed

00:10:57.865 --> 00:10:59.909
over on the other side of my field."

00:10:59.909 --> 00:11:01.327
No. If you see a fireball,

00:11:01.327 --> 00:11:03.162
it's actually really far away,

00:11:03.162 --> 00:11:05.331
because it's very high in the atmosphere.

00:11:05.331 --> 00:11:07.833
Woo-hoo, I'm on fire!

00:11:07.833 --> 00:11:08.668
Woo-hoo!

00:11:08.668 --> 00:11:10.961
You would not be catching fireballs.

00:11:10.961 --> 00:11:14.465
And if a fireball hit you
like it hit those two,

00:11:14.465 --> 00:11:16.634
you would be fried.

00:11:16.634 --> 00:11:19.053
You'd be in the crater
that was left behind.

00:11:19.053 --> 00:11:21.263
From the perspective of
science, it's not realistic.

00:11:21.263 --> 00:11:22.640
So I give this a,

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:24.058
I think a 1 out of 10.

00:11:28.688 --> 00:11:30.356
From the perspective of

00:11:30.356 --> 00:11:32.316
a rock hitting the ground
that came from space,

00:11:32.316 --> 00:11:33.567
yes, that's a meteorite.

00:11:33.567 --> 00:11:35.611
Honey, bring your phone. C'mon, c'mon!

00:11:35.611 --> 00:11:37.697
Don't go near it! Don't go near it!

00:11:37.697 --> 00:11:39.240
It glowing on the ground,

00:11:39.240 --> 00:11:40.241
no, that's wrong.

00:11:40.241 --> 00:11:42.076
So that means there's
something inside of it.

00:11:42.076 --> 00:11:44.870
It might glow as it's coming
through the atmosphere,

00:11:44.870 --> 00:11:46.539
because the chemistry of the rock

00:11:46.539 --> 00:11:47.873
might interact with the atmosphere.

00:11:47.873 --> 00:11:49.834
So you could get
different colors that way.

00:11:49.834 --> 00:11:51.711
Purple would tell you something about

00:11:51.711 --> 00:11:53.504
the elements that are in it.

00:11:53.504 --> 00:11:55.589
Meteors that glow different colors,

00:11:55.589 --> 00:11:58.175
but again, those are just
small pieces of dust.

00:11:58.175 --> 00:11:59.093
Smells.

00:12:00.553 --> 00:12:03.806
The amount of smoke coming
out of it, like a barbecue,

00:12:03.806 --> 00:12:06.225
that is not something that you would see.

00:12:06.225 --> 00:12:08.102
A meteorite coming through the atmosphere,

00:12:08.102 --> 00:12:11.313
it heats up, but only the outside.

00:12:11.313 --> 00:12:14.483
So when it hits the ground,
it's actually really cold,

00:12:14.483 --> 00:12:17.194
because space is cold and
it's been out in space.

00:12:17.194 --> 00:12:21.115
And there have been reports
of people finding meteorites

00:12:21.115 --> 00:12:22.700
very shortly after they've fallen

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:25.453
and them having ice on them.

00:12:25.453 --> 00:12:27.496
And if you're going to get burned from it,

00:12:27.496 --> 00:12:31.542
it's going to be freezer
burn, not a heat burn.

00:12:31.542 --> 00:12:33.335
You can get close to them

00:12:33.335 --> 00:12:36.756
as long as they haven't
come in at a massive speed.

00:12:36.756 --> 00:12:39.133
Peekskill meteorite in the US,

00:12:39.133 --> 00:12:41.844
October 1992,

00:12:41.844 --> 00:12:45.264
the meteor was seen
across about 16 states.

00:12:45.264 --> 00:12:49.810
It actually hit the boot
of a car in New York

00:12:49.810 --> 00:12:52.354
and punched a hole through the car.

00:12:55.149 --> 00:12:56.525
Did you plant those?

00:12:58.152 --> 00:12:59.695
No.

00:12:59.695 --> 00:13:03.908
It would not be something
that is actually alive

00:13:03.908 --> 00:13:07.244
after having gone through
that atmospheric thing,

00:13:07.244 --> 00:13:09.622
unless the meteorite
is somehow a spaceship,

00:13:09.622 --> 00:13:12.208
which, generally they're just rocks.

00:13:12.208 --> 00:13:14.293
The idea that there could
be life on a meteorite,

00:13:14.293 --> 00:13:17.713
that's one area of study in our world.

00:13:17.713 --> 00:13:20.007
We check for evidence

00:13:20.007 --> 00:13:22.259
of either organic material

00:13:22.259 --> 00:13:27.139
or the precursors of organic things

00:13:27.139 --> 00:13:29.517
that could potentially harbor life.

00:13:29.517 --> 00:13:31.477
We haven't found any so far.

00:13:31.477 --> 00:13:34.438
So we've found precursor materials,

00:13:34.438 --> 00:13:37.358
but no actual evidence of life.

00:13:37.358 --> 00:13:38.609
The color,

00:13:38.609 --> 00:13:41.195
it doesn't glow on the ground at all.

00:13:41.195 --> 00:13:44.532
I'm going with zero out of 10 on this one.

00:13:49.703 --> 00:13:50.788
This is the comet,

00:13:50.788 --> 00:13:53.082
and it is hitting water.

00:13:53.082 --> 00:13:54.792
The odds of anything hitting water

00:13:54.792 --> 00:13:56.335
are really high on the Earth.

00:13:59.588 --> 00:14:02.550
That tsunami wave looks very high to me,

00:14:02.550 --> 00:14:04.885
but I think you could get a tsunami wave

00:14:04.885 --> 00:14:06.762
that was something like 600 meters high

00:14:06.762 --> 00:14:08.264
with the size of that object.

00:14:11.809 --> 00:14:13.769
The running-away part, in general,

00:14:13.769 --> 00:14:15.938
you're going to have some time

00:14:15.938 --> 00:14:18.107
before the tsunami hits.

00:14:18.107 --> 00:14:21.694
As we know from recent
events with volcanoes

00:14:21.694 --> 00:14:23.153
in the middle of the Pacific,

00:14:23.153 --> 00:14:24.989
it takes a long time for the tsunami

00:14:24.989 --> 00:14:26.782
to get to wherever it's going.

00:14:26.782 --> 00:14:30.327
If the impact is upon
you, you have no warning.

00:14:30.327 --> 00:14:33.247
The only way that happens
is if we haven't tracked it.

00:14:33.247 --> 00:14:35.457
And the only way that happens
is if we're not looking.

00:14:35.457 --> 00:14:37.126
And so none of those things are happening.

00:14:42.506 --> 00:14:46.802
You wouldn't have this
level of water coming inland

00:14:46.802 --> 00:14:48.929
as far as I think this is.

00:14:48.929 --> 00:14:53.017
So even if the impact had
happened very close to shore,

00:14:53.017 --> 00:14:54.852
the depths of the water would be less,

00:14:54.852 --> 00:14:56.353
so the wave would be lower.

00:14:56.353 --> 00:14:57.897
This is completely ... no.

00:14:57.897 --> 00:15:02.026
I would probably head for
the middle of a continent

00:15:02.026 --> 00:15:04.403
if I knew it was going to hit water.

00:15:04.403 --> 00:15:08.991
"Deep Impact," for realism,
I'd give it a 1 out of 10.

00:15:11.869 --> 00:15:14.496
Why does the ephemeris keep
getting lower and lower?

00:15:16.957 --> 00:15:18.834
Professor Mindy?

00:15:18.834 --> 00:15:20.002
So, this is really good.

00:15:20.002 --> 00:15:21.420
I like this aspect of it,

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:23.881
is we're going to run through the numbers

00:15:23.881 --> 00:15:25.466
and we're going to do the calculations

00:15:25.466 --> 00:15:26.842
from first principles.

00:15:26.842 --> 00:15:30.179
0.0 means we're getting
close to the Earth.

00:15:30.179 --> 00:15:31.430
That doesn't mean it's going to hit,

00:15:31.430 --> 00:15:36.018
'cause 0.05 AU is still
farther away than the moon.

00:15:36.018 --> 00:15:37.853
The way we currently check for things

00:15:37.853 --> 00:15:39.688
that might be potentially hazardous

00:15:39.688 --> 00:15:41.649
is when we do these calculations,

00:15:41.649 --> 00:15:43.233
we work out, well, if it's going to be

00:15:43.233 --> 00:15:46.028
closer than 0.05 AU from the Earth,

00:15:46.028 --> 00:15:47.738
then we call it potentially hazardous.

00:15:47.738 --> 00:15:50.074
There's a good chance it'll never hit.

00:15:50.074 --> 00:15:54.036
So his worry is probably

00:15:54.036 --> 00:15:55.454
not unfounded.

00:15:55.454 --> 00:15:59.792
It happens several times a
year that you'll get something

00:15:59.792 --> 00:16:03.462
at this 0.05 or less.

00:16:03.462 --> 00:16:05.631
But generally, the sizes of the objects

00:16:05.631 --> 00:16:08.884
are only a few meters or
tens of meters across.

00:16:18.936 --> 00:16:23.065
This particular size comet
would not completely decimate.

00:16:23.065 --> 00:16:24.775
It would not take out the Earth.

00:16:24.775 --> 00:16:26.860
It would destroy it, but
it wouldn't break it apart.

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:27.820
It would make it a mess,

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:30.614
but it wouldn't even
wobble it off its orbit.

00:16:30.614 --> 00:16:33.617
I think they said it's,
like, 10 to 15 miles across.

00:16:33.617 --> 00:16:35.327
Even the moon-forming event,

00:16:35.327 --> 00:16:40.165
which was a Mars-sized object
smacking into the Earth,

00:16:40.165 --> 00:16:41.792
did not destroy the Earth.

00:16:41.792 --> 00:16:43.585
The probability of something

00:16:43.585 --> 00:16:47.297
the size of what these things show

00:16:47.297 --> 00:16:52.261
actually hitting is so
infinitesimally small at this point,

00:16:52.261 --> 00:16:54.763
because the solar system
has evolved over time.

00:16:54.763 --> 00:16:56.015
So don't be afraid.

00:16:56.015 --> 00:16:58.517
We're doing a really good
job of tracking things.

00:16:58.517 --> 00:17:01.562
We have a much better handle
on how we look out in space.

00:17:01.562 --> 00:17:02.771
Thanks so much for watching.

00:17:02.771 --> 00:17:05.566
